A||N||2||DaveL||Dogbone||||1||
Z||000000||DaveL||10-31-2000||01:39 PM||||How important is it for the dogbone to be centered in its range of adjustment? I have an 87 SE 2.5 auto.||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||10-31-2000||03:35 PM||||I don't know if its critical per se, but you want to be careful that whatever movement the bone allows doesn't stress components or result in the air cleaner hitting the trunk wall.<P>Probably the best way to install the dogbone is to park the car on a flat surface, loosen the bone bolts and allow the engine to find its center and then tighten the bolts.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-31-2000||08:06 PM||||If that's a stock strut, push the engine all the way forward. The excessive load on the bone will allow the air cleaner to hit the trunk wall if you don't. (Even then it might when you floor it.) This engine setup won't settle to a self center like the others will. I push the engine all the way forward with the polly strut too.<P>Read the article in my cave about that tranny setup to understand just why it is a problem better. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>http://fierocave.shorturl.com</A>  ||||reg||1
